  "question": {
    "text": "If the lines $\\frac{x+1}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{1}=\\frac{z+1}{3}$ and $\\frac{x+2}{2}=\\frac{y-k}{3}=\\frac{z}{4}$ are coplanar, then the value of $k$ is :",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "gnkGq",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{11}{2}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "ZA7WU",
      "text": "\n$-\\frac{11}{2}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "hi7Lh",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{9}{2}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "qTTCz",
      "text": "\n$-\\frac{9}{2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Two given planes are coplanar, if\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\left|\\begin{array}{ccc}\n-2-(-1) & k-1 & 0-(-1) \\\\\n2 & 1 & 3 \\\\\n2 & 3 & 4\n\\end{array}\\right|=0 \\\\\n\\Rightarrow & \\left|\\begin{array}{ccc}\n-1 & k-1 & 1 \\\\\n2 & 1 & 3 \\\\\n2 & 3 & 4\n\\end{array}\\right|=0 \\\\\n\\Rightarrow & (-1)(4-9)-(k-1)(8-6)+6-2=0 \\\\\n\\Rightarrow & k=\\frac{11}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    "image": null
  },
